            <ArticleTitle>Back pain in patients with ductal pancreatic cancer. Its impact on resectability and prognosis after resection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1216-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Back pain is a frequent and often ominous clinical sign in patients with ductal pancreatic cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">From 1971 to 1993 a pancreatic carcinoma could be resected in 192 patients, whereas 261 patients underwent either probatory laparotomy alone or palliative bypass procedures. In a retrospective study including uni- and multi-variate survival analysis we have determined the impact of preoperative back pain on both resectability and long-term prognosis after resection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Among the presenting symptoms of patients with ductal pancreatic cancer back pain was a predictive sign of irresectability. In the presence of preoperative back pain the long-term prognosis after resection of the tumour was also significantly impaired. In a multivariate analysis it could be demonstrated that the prognostic impact of back pain was as strong as the influence of residual tumour, tumour grading, and tumour size.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Back pain often indicates irresectability of ductal pancreatic carcinoma and also impairs the long-term prognosis even after curative resection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
